Assemble Kitchen Tables
Assemble Kitchen Tables
In this basic sample model we demonstrate how to produce two different types of tables and show how to
attach the table legs to the table tops.
The Source named TableTopsIn produces two brown table tops and two white table tops. The Source
named TableLegsIn produces four table legs with different colors. The sources send the table tops and
table legs on to the AssemblyStation which in turn attaches the respective legs to the matching table tops.
It then sends the assembled tables on to shipping station, which we modeled with a Drain.
We will demonstrate how to:
• Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
• Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
Compare Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
Back to Assemble Parts with the AssemblyStation
Assemble Kitchen Tables
Plant Simulation Help 10-587
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
We inserted and configured the Sources, which produce the parts, the table tops and table legs. The
AssemblyStation then assembles the table.
To create the simulation model, we inserted the objects so that they match the screenshot below.
We inserted and configured the Sources, which are to produce the parts, the table tops and table legs in
our case:
• We selected the type of part which the Source named TableTopsIn is to produce. We selected the MU
Selection > Sequence Cyclical and then selected the name of the Sequence Table, which we inserted
before, namely the DataTable named TableTops. Selecting the table automatically applied the correct
format.
Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
10-588 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• We entered the required data into the Sequence Table.
We dragged the MU named TableTop, which we created and stored in the folder Models, to the cell
MU and dropped it there.
As we want to create two brown table tops, we entered 2 as the Number. We then entered the name
of the part, namely TableTop_A, which also is the name of the icon of the brown table top into the
Attributes subtable, which we named a1.
We repeated this for our white table top.
Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
Plant Simulation Help 10-589
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• We selected the type of part which the source TableLegsIn is to produce. We selected the MU selection
> Sequence Cyclical and then selected the name of the Sequence Table, which we inserted before,
namely the DataTable named TableLegs. Selecting the table automatically applied the correct format.
• We entered the required data into the Sequence Table.
We dragged the MU named TableLeg, which we created and stored in the folder Models, to the cell
MU and dropped it there.
As we want to create two sets of legs for our brown table tops, we entered 8 as the Number. We then
entered the name of the part, namely Leg_A into the Attributes subtable, which we named a1.
Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
10-590 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We repeated this for the legs that go with our white table top.
We inserted and configured the AssemblyStation, which is going to attach the matching table legs to the
table tops.
• As we want to attach the table legs to the table tops, we selected Assembly Table > Predecessors.
• We then clicked Open and entered the number of the predecessor from which the legs arrive, 2 in our
case, and the number of parts want to attach, 4 in our case.
Create the Assembly of the Kitchen Tables
Plant Simulation Help 10-591
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• As the Assembly Mode we selected Attach MUs.
• As the part that the AssemblyStation is going to pass on is the finished table, we selected Main MU,
which arrived from predecessor 1.
When we start the simulation, we can watch how the AssemblyStation attaches the four blue legs to the
brown table top, passes the assembled table on to the shipping station, and repeats this once. It then
attaches four orange legs to the white table top, passes the assembled table on to the shipping station,
and does the same again, and then repeats the sequence, namely two brown table tops, followed by two
white table tops, and so on.
Note
If the assembly process does not work as expected, check the sequence in which you connected its
predecessors with the AssemblyStation. Dragging the mouse over a Connector shows the number of the
Connector in the tooltip. Also make sure that the Main MU arrives from the correct predecessor.
Back to Assemble Kitchen Tables
