• For the table legs we entered these settings for the MU Size for the class of the TableLeg.
• For the table tops we entered these settings for the MU Size for the class of the TableTop.
• The result with the adjusted dimensions of the standard graphics of the part and the pallet look like this:
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
Plant Simulation Help 10-593
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

As our tables do not look like tables, we create graphics for the varicolored table legs and table tops.
Proceed as follows to create the blue and orange table legs:
• Right-click the object TableLeg in the Class Library and select Open in 3D.
• Press the spacebar and change to the tab Graphics in the dialog Edit 3D Properties. Add a graphic
group with the name Leg_A and Leg_B each.
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
10-594 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Delete the graphic named default. Change to the Edit tab and click Cylinder under Insert Shape. Select
the graphic group for which you want to create the graphic. We selected these settings for Leg_A:
• This inserts the Cylinder at the origin of the scene. It does not have the right color though.
• Click the Cylinder and press the spacebar. Change to the Material tab, select Material Active, and select
a color. Our Leg_A is blue.
• Repeat these steps for Leg_B and select Orange as the color.
Proceed as follows to create the brown and the white table tops:
• Right-click the object TableTop in the Class Library and select Open in 3D.
• Press the spacebar and change to the tab Graphics in the dialog Edit 3D Properties. Add a graphic
group with the name TableTop_A and TableTop_B each.
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
Plant Simulation Help 10-595
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Delete the graphic named default. Change to the Edit tab and click Cuboid under Insert Shape. Select
the graphic group for which you want to create the graphic. We selected these settings for TableTop_A:
• This inserts the flat cuboid at the origin of the scene. It does not have the right color yet.
• Click the Cuboid and press the spacebar. Change to the Material tab, select Material Active, and select
a color. Our TableTop_A is brown.
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
10-596 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Repeat these steps for TableTop_B and select a non all white tone.
When we run the simulation, we see that the AssemblyStation assembles the varicolored tables correctly
but does not show them in the correct colors.
Plant Simulation shows them according to the graphic group names that we entered. To accomplish this,
we create a user-defined attribute and an observer for the user-defined attribute in the class of the object.
• In the class of the table leg we created an observer for the user-defined attribute named
PictureName3D, which detects when a table leg with another name arrives, and then displays the
correct graphic.
• We entered this source code into the method of the observer OnPictureName3DChanged:
// param attribute: string, oldValue: string, newValue: string
// param attribut: string, oldValue: string
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
Plant Simulation Help 10-597
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

param newValue: string
self.~._3D.VisibleGraphicGroups := [newValue]
• We then enter the name of the user-defined attribute named PictureName3D and the name of the
respective graphic group into the two sub-tables named a1 and a2 of the DataTable named TableLegs.
• In the class of the table top we created an observer for the user-defined attribute
PictureName3DTableTop, which detects when a table top with another name arrives, and then
displays the correct graphic.
• We entered this source code into the method of the observer OnPictureName3DTableTopChanged:
// param Attribute: string, oldValue: string, newValue: string
// param Attribut: string, oldValue: string
param newValue: string
self.~._3D.VisibleGraphicGroups := [newValue]
• We then enter the name of the user-defined attribute named PictureName3DTableTop and the name
of the respective graphic group into the two sub-tables named a1 and a2 of the DataTable named
TableTops.
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
10-598 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When we now run the simulation, we see that two tables each with a brown and two tables each with
a bright-colored table top are produced. When zooming the AssemblyStation we see that the table top is
not positioned flat on the assembly area, but with an offset to the top. In addition the left legs jut into the
assembly boxes at the back of the AssemblyStation.
This is easy to correct:
• Click the AssemblyStation and press the spacebar. Change to the tab MU Animation.
• Show the path named default.
Adjust the Graphics of the Assembly
Plant Simulation Help 10-599
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Click Edit and change the values as shown in the figure so that the table top lies flat on the assembly
table and that the legs do not touch the assembly boxes any longer.
